## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:  Cellulose  Hydrogel  Slow-release
In this work, a kind of MOF MIL-100(Fe)@CNFs hydrogel (MC) based on TEMPO-oxidized cellulose nanofibers (CNFs) for fertilizers slow-release was prepared by free-radical polymerization, where \(N\)-vinyl caprolactam (NVCL) and CNFs were involved to exhibit temperature and pH response, respectively. Particularly, porous MIL-100(Fe), a kind of metal organic frameworks (MOFs), was introduced to optimize the load and slow-release capabilities. The Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy, X-ray diffraction, scanning electron microscopy, and thermogravimetric analysis were used to characterize. The swelling behaviors and water-retention capabilities of hydrogels were evaluated. Using urea as the model fertilizer, the slow-release mechanism was revealed. Wheat was used as the model crop to evaluate the practical growth status. Compared with MC-0% hydrogels, the MC-10% hydrogels exhibited a better swelling capacity (37 g/g), water-retention (22.78%) and slow-release performance (40.84%). It also exhibited sensitivities to temperature and pH for regulating urea release. Besides, the number of tillers and leaves of wheat fertilized with MC hydrogels significantly increased, as did the photosynthetic rate. In conclusion, the MC-0% hydrogels had a positive influence on crops growth, and promoted the possible utilization of hydrogels in slow-release fertilizers.
## 1. Introduction
The efficient use of fertilizers and water resources can greatly increase crops yields, and meet increased demands. However, the usage efficiencies of conventional fertilizers (urea) are generally \(<35\%\) in developing countries, and the majority of nitrogen cannot be absorbed by crops [1,2]. The inefficient use of fertilizers not only causes economic losses, but also leads to a series of environmental problems [3]. Recent studies have reported that the development of slow-release fertilizers can significantly improve the utilization rates of fertilizers and water, as well as promote steady crop growth [4–7].
Recently, metal-organic frameworks composed of metal ions and organic ligands [8] have attracted significant interest in various fields, such as catalysis and drug delivery systems, which can be attributed to their abundant pore structures and guest loading abilities [9]. Although
Recently, metal-organic frameworks composed of metal ions and organic ligands [8] have attracted significant interest in various fields, such as catalysis and drug delivery systems, which can be attributed to their abundant pore structures and guest loading abilities [9]. Although MOFs have the disadvantage of relatively expensive at present, there are great opportunities for achieving MOFs production costs of less than $10/kg in the future [10]. MOF MIL-100(Fe) has been reported as a typical MOF with a large pore structure and zeotype architecture [11]. Therefore, it has been considered that MOF MIL-100(Fe) possess great potential to load fertilizers and water for crop growth. However, the applied performances of MOF MIL-100(Fe) remain limited by their disadvantageous nature, such as intrinsic fragility and unsatisfactory stability. Besides, they cannot achieve slow release without a carrier [12]. Thus, MOF-based hydrogels receive much attention [13,14]. The three-dimensional (3D) networks of hydrogels can be used to promote the dispersibility and stability of MOFs. In addition, MOFs can be used to tune the properties of hydrogels, and as a result, MOF-based hydrogels exhibit the advantages of both MOFs and hydrogels [15,16].
Hydrogels have been widely studied for slow-release applications
owing to their 3D networks [17] and abundant hydrophilic groups [18], which can absorb and retain water. In particular, stimuli-responsive hydrogels [19] have grown in popularity since their swelling and release behaviors can be regulated according to external environmental conditions, such as variations in temperature, pH, and light [20,21]. A number of studies have examined the application of environmental (temperature and pH) responsive hydrogels for slow-release fertilizers [22] due to the importance of soil temperature and pH during crop growth. Indeed, reports have demonstrated that using hydrogels with temperature- and pH-responsive as water and fertilizer carriers can lead to desired nutrient release rates [23]. In such systems, the release performance is regulated according to external temperature and pH [24].
Nowadays, with the growing attention paid to environmental protection, bio-based hydrogels have attracted great attention [25–26]. Moreover, reports on the controlled release of fertilizers have proved that natural polymers such as chitosan, cellulose and alginates, have an excellent effect of controlling nutrient release and are environmentally friendly [27–31]. Among the various available natural polymer materials, cellulose has the advantages of a wide range of sources, a low price, and an excellent biodegradability [32–35]. Besides, TEMPO-oxidized nanocellulose (CNFs) [36], a kind of cellulose derivative, not only possesses abundant hydroxyl groups to form hydrogen bonds during the cross-linking process, but also has rich carboxyl groups that can provide pH-responsiveness [37]. In addition, cellulose-based hydrogels exhibiting dual-responsiveness can be constructed by introducing the temperature-sensitive \(N\!\cdot\) -vinyl caprolactam (NVCL) monomer. It can therefore be hypothesized that adulterating MOF MIL-100(Fe) into dual-responsive hydrogels will contribute to optimize the adsorption capacity and slow-release performance due to the characteristics of the rich pore structure of MOF MIL-100(Fe).
Herein, we report the preparation of the MOF MIL-100(Fe)@CNFs hydrogels. The hydrogels were carried out by combining MOF MIL100(Fe) with a temperature and pH dual-responsive hydrogels based on cellulose nanofibers (Fig. 1). NVCL was used as a temperature-sensitive monomer [38], while the CNFs provided pH-responsiveness [39]. The structure of the hydrogels was characterized by FT-IR, XRD, and SEM. The swelling ratio, urea release behavior, temperature-responsive nature, and pH-responsive properties of the MC hydrogel were investigated. Finally, the obtained MC hydrogels were applied in the growth of wheat.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
2,2,6,6-Tetramethylpiperidine-1-oxyl (TEMPO, \(98\%\) ), 1,3,5-
benzenetricarboxylic acid \(\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C}\) , \(98\%\) ), ammonium persulfate (APS, \(98\%\) ), sodium hypochlorite (NaClO, \(14\%\) ), \(N{,}N\) -dimethylacrylamide (DMAA, \(98\%\) ), \(N\!\cdot\) -vinyl caprolactam (NVCL, \(99\%\) ), and \(N{,}N\) -methylenebisacrylamide (MBA, \(99\%\) ) were purchased from Aladdin Chemical Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Sodium hydroxide (NaOH, \(97\%\) ), \(N{,}N\cdot\) - dimethylformamide (DMF, \(99.5\%\) ), hydrochloric acid (HCl), sodium alginate (SA), and urea were purchased from Nanjing Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Nanjing, China). Eucalyptus pulp was purchased from ARAUCO Co., Ltd. (Chile).
## 2.2. Synthesis of the TEMPO-oxidized cellulose nanofibers (CNFs)
NaBr \(_{(0.5\,g)}\) and TEMPO \(_{(0.08\;\mathrm{g})}\) were dissolved in deionized water \(\mathrm{(500\,mL)}\) and mechanically stirred in a three-neck round-bottomed flask \(\mathrm{[000\,mL)}\) . After adding the previously pulverized eucalyptus pulp \(\left(5\,\mathrm{g}\right)\) , a solution of NaClO \(\mathrm{[8.5\,mL}\) , pH 10), whose pH value was adjusted using a \(0.1\;\mathrm{mol/L}\) HCl solution, was slowly added to the flask at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . A 0.1 mol/L NaOH solution was added at the same time to maintain the pH of the system at \({\sim}10\) . The CNFs were obtained via a suction filtration purification process, in which the CNFs were washed with deionized water until the conductivity of the washed solution was \({<}20~\upmu\mathrm{S/cm}\) . Finally, pure CNFs were mixed with sodium alginate in deionized water to prepare the CNFs-SA solution used in the next step.
## 2.3. Synthesis of MOF MIL-100(Fe)
MOF MIL-100(Fe) was synthesized via a hydrothermal route. \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}\bullet6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (3.1 mmol) and \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) (3.1 mmol) were added to DMF (60 \(\mathrm{mL})\) to form a uniformly-mixed solution at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Subsequently, an aliquot \(\mathrm{(30\,mL)}\) of the mixture was transferred to a reactor and heated at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) . The desired MOF MIL-100(Fe) was obtained after being washed with ethanol and dried in an oven at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .
## 2.4. Synthesis of the MC hydrogels
The MC hydrogels were prepared by the free-radical polymerization of NVCL and DMAA in the presence of CNFs-SA and MOF MIL-100(Fe). NVCL was dispersed evenly in DMAA in an ice bath to form solution I, then MOF MIL-100(Fe) and solution II were mixed with the CNFs-SA solution to form solution II. Subsequently, APS (initiator) and MBA (cross-linking agent) were added to solution II under continuous stirring. The obtained mixture was incubated at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{5\,\mathrm{h}}\) in a mold. The residual unreacted monomer was removed by being washed with deionized water, and the resulting hydrogel was obtained by being dried using a freeze dryer. The components used for this preparation are listed in Table S1.
## 2.5. Material characterization
The structures of the monomers and hydrogels were analyzed using Fourier transform infrared spectrometry (FTIR) with a scan range of 4000 to \(400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) . The crystallinity was investigated by X-ray diffraction (XRD) at a scan rate of \(0.2^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}\) , and the crystal structure was determined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was performed to observe the surface morphology. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was used to measure the thermal stabilities of the freeze-dried hydrogels \((5{-}10~\mathrm{mg})\) from 40 to \(700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area was obtained using an automatic specific surface area and physical adsorption analyzer.
## 2.6. Swelling behavior
The swelling behaviors of the hydrogels were measured using the gravimetric method. More specifically, the freeze-dried hydrogels were immersed in deionized water to attain a swelling equilibrium at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .
The swollen hydrogels were then removed, and their weights were recorded after removing any excess water. The swelling ratios (SR, \( \mathbf{g}/\mathbf{g} \)) of hydrogels at a specific time was calculated as follows:
\[
SR=(w_{t}-w_{0})/w_{0}
\]
where \( w_{0} \) (g) and \( w_{t} \) (g) represent the weights of the dried hydrogels and the swollen hydrogels at a specific time \( t \) (h), respectively.
To study the reusability of the hydrogels, dried hydrogels \( (w_{0}) \) were immersed in distilled water for \( 5\,\mathrm{h} \) and then were weighed \( (w_{1}) \). Subsequently, the hydrogels were placed in a constant temperature box \( (30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\,30\% \) humidity) to release water, and were weighed again. The water absorption content was calculated as follows:
\[
MC=(w_{t}-w_{0})/w_{0}
\]
## 2.7. Temperature and pH responsiveness
To characterize the temperature-responsive behaviors of the prepared hydrogels, their equilibrium swelling ratios (ESR) were measured by immersing the dried hydrogels in deionized water at different temperatures (5, 15, 25, 35, 45, and \( 55\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \)). The weights of the swollen hydrogels were recorded after removing excess water. The formula for calculating the ESR \( (\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{g}) \) was as follows:
\[
ESR=(w_{e}-w_{0})/w_{0}
\]
where \( w_{0} \) (g) and \( w_{e} \) (g) represent the weights of the dried hydrogels and hydrogels at the swelling equilibrium, respectively.
To understand the cyclic temperature-responsiveness properties of the hydrogels, experiments were carried out at 25 and \( 55\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) as follows: The initial hydrogels \( (w_{0}) \) were immersed in deionized water for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \) at \( 25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). The weights \( (w_{t}) \) were then recorded. Subsequently, the hydrogels were placed in deionized water for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \) at \( 50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), and after being weighed, the hydrogels were immersed at \( 25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \). Water absorption (WA) was calculated using the following equation:
\[
WA=(w_{t}-w_{0})/w_{0}
\]
To characterize the pH-responsive and cyclic pH-responsiveness behaviors of the hydrogels, similar experimental procedure was carried out with pH varies from 3 to 11 and in deionized water \( \mathrm{pH}=3 \) and 11) at \( 25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) respectively.
## 2.8. Water-retention behavior
The water-retention behaviors of the hydrogels in deionized water were studied using the gravimetric method. Initially, the dried hydrogels \( (w_{0}) \) were immersed in deionized water at \( 25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) and were weighed when the swelling equilibrium was attained \( (w_{e}) \). Subsequently, the swollen hydrogels were placed at a higher temperature \( (55\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}) \). The swollen hydrogels were removed from water at predetermined times \( (w_{t}) \), and the weights were noted after removing any excess water. The water-retention (WR, \( \mathbf{g}/\mathbf{g} \)) was calculated as follows:
\[
WR=(w_{t}-w_{0})/w_{e}\times100\%
\]
The water-retaining ratio \( (W_{r}) \) in the soil was obtained according to the method of previous literature [40]. The mixture of freeze-dried hydrogels (0.5 and \( 1.0\,\mathrm{g} \)) and dried sand soil \( (100\,\mathrm{g}) \) was placed into a beaker \( (250\,\mathrm{mL}) \), and a sample of dried sand soil \( (100\,\mathrm{g}) \) containing no samples was prepared as a control. The beakers were weighed to obtain their initial weights \( (w) \), and distilled water \( (30\,\mathrm{mL}) \) was added \( (w_{0}) \) gradually. Finally, the beakers were weighed \( (w_{t}) \) at regular time intervals until a constant weight was achieved. The \( W_{r} \) (\%) of the hydrogel in the soil can be determined using the following equation:
\[
W_{r}=(w_{t}-w)/(w_{0}-w)\times100\%
\]
The maximum water holding rate in the soil was also measured. For
this purpose, the mixture of freeze-dried hydrogels (0, 0.5, and \( 1.0\,\mathrm{g} \)) and dried sand soil \( (100\,\mathrm{g}) \) were placed in a PVC pipe and were weighed to obtain the initial weight \( (w_{0}) \) after sealing the bottom of the pipe with nylon cloth. Subsequently, tap water was added slowly from the top layer of the soil until the water seeped out from the bottom, and the setup was weighted once again when there was no water seeping from the bottom \( (w_{1}) \). The \( H_{r} \) (\%) of the hydrogel in the soil was calculated as follows:
\[
H_{r}=(w_{1}-w_{0})/w_{0}\times100\%
\]
## 2.9. Slow-release behavior
Load and release experiments were carried out on the \( \mathrm{MC}{-}0\% \) and \( \mathrm{MC}{-}10\% \) hydrogels using urea as a fertilizer model. To obtain hydrogels loaded with urea, the dried hydrogels were dipped into the aqueous urea solution \( (25\,\mathrm{mg/mL}) \) for \( 72\,\mathrm{h} \) at \( 25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). To investigate their urea release behaviors, hydrogels loaded with urea were placed in water at different temperatures and pH values. In each case, the soaking solution \( (1\,\mathrm{mL}) \) was collected at regular intervals, and the amount of urea released was characterized using UV–visible spectrophotometry. The cumulative release rate (\%) of urea was calculated as follows:
\[
Cummulative~release~(\%)=m_t/m_0\times100\%=\frac{\mathrm{mt}}{\mathrm{ml}}\times100\%
\]
where \( m_{t} \) (g) and \( m_{0} \) (g) represent the quantity of urea released at a certain time and the urea loading, respectively.
To investigate their release behaviors in soil, the urea-loaded hydrogels \( (1\,\mathrm{g}) \) were buried in dry sand \( (0.50\,\mathrm{g}) \) at a depth of approximately \( 5{-}6\,\mathrm{cm} \) below the surface for \( 30\,\mathrm{d} \) at room temperature. During the experiment, distilled water \( (200\,\mathrm{mL}) \) was irrigated every \( 5\,\mathrm{d} \). Three sets of experiments were performed in parallel. Samples of the soil (1.5 g) were removed at different periods (1, 3, 5, 7, 10, 15, 20, 25, and 30 d) and the cumulative amount of urea released was calculated using the Kjeldahl method, which can indicate the total nitrogen content present in the soil. The cumulative release rate (\%) of urea was calculated using Eq. (8).
To elucidate the urea release mechanism in soil, four kinetic mathematical models (i.e., the zero-order, first-order, Higuchi, and Korsmeyer-Peppas models) were used to fit and analyze the data obtained [4,41,42]:
\[
\textcircled{1}\mathrm{Zero-order~release~kinetics~model:}M_{t}/M_{\infty}=k_{0}\mathfrak{t}
\]
\[
\textcircled{2}\mathrm{First-order~release~kinetics~model:}M_{t}/M_{\infty}=1-\mathrm{e}^{-k_{1}t}
\]
\[
\textcircled{3}\mathrm{The~Higuchi~model:}M_{t}/M_{\infty}=k_{\mathrm{H}}t^{1/2}
\]
were performed, through which one wheat group was treated with urea only, and the other was treated with the pure hydrogel. Untreated naturally dried soil and the dried hydrogel loaded with urea were mixed in the experimental pot ( \(14\,\mathrm{cm}\) diameter, \(50\,\mathrm{cm}\) length), and water (200 mL) was added to the experimental pot every \(5\,\mathrm{d}\).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of the CNFs, MOF MIL-100(Fe), and the MC hydrogels
The preparation process employed for the MOF MIL-100(Fe)@CNFs (MC) hydrogels was shown in Fig. 2. More specifically, carboxylated nanocellulose was prepared using TEMPO (Fig. 2a) and the carboxyl groups of the CNFs provided pH-responsiveness. The FTIR spectra of the prepared CNFs can be seen in Fig. 3a, where the presence of a peak at \(1600\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) associated to the stretching vibration of \(\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}\) indicated that the hydroxyl groups at position \(C_{6}\) were successfully converted to carboxylate groups. Structural analysis of the CNFs was carried out by XRD (Fig. 3c), wherein the peaks at 14–16, 23, and \(35^{\circ}\) correspond to the (1 1 0), (2 0 0), and (2 0 4) crystal planes, respectively.
Owing to its superior crosslinking ability, SA was mixed with the CNFs to form a CNFs-SA solution and to enhance the strength of the
hydrogels. The FTIR spectrum of SA was shown in Fig. S1. To obtain hydrogels with high adsorption capacities and improved slow-release capabilities, the porous MOF MIL-100(Fe) was obtained by hydrothermal synthesis (Fig. 2b). In the FTIR spectrum of MOF MIL-100(Fe), the absorption peaks at 1640 and \(1384~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) correspond to the stretching vibration of the carboxylate \(\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}\) and \(\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}\) bonds, respectively, while the sharp peaks at 760 and \(712~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can be attributed to the \(\mathrm{C{-}H}\) bending vibrations of benzene. Furthermore, the presence of an obvious absorption peak at \(3400~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) indicated that there may be a large amount of crystal water or coordinated water present in the MIL-100 (Fe) structure. The major diffraction peaks associated with MOF MIL\(100(\mathrm{Fe})\) appeared at 4.2 and \(10.8^{\circ}\) (Fig. 3c). Overall, these observations confirmed the successful preparation of MOF MIL-100(Fe).
DMAA is a commonly used co-monomer of NVCL for the preparation of hydrogels. Thus, for the purpose of this study, NVCL was mixed evenly with DMAA to form solution I (Fig. 2c), which was subsequently added to the CNFs-SA solution along with MOF MIL-100(Fe). It can be seen from the resulting FTIR spectrum that the peaks at 1681, 3108, and 992 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), corresponding to the double bond \(\scriptstyle\left(-\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}-\right)\) and vinyl peaks \(\scriptstyle\left(=\mathrm{CH}\right.\) and \(\scriptstyle=\mathrm{CH}_{2}\) ) of NVCL, disappeared in the \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{-}0\%\) and \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}10\%\) hydrogels. In addition, as can be seen from the XPS spectra (Fig. 3d), N 1s peaks were observed for the \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{-}0\%\) and \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}10\%\) hydrogels, thereby indicating the successful introduction of NVCL and DMAA. Besides, the peaks at 2.87 ppm and 3.21 ppm shown in \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectra (Fig. S2) were related to the proton of \(\scriptstyle-\mathbf{N}-\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{3}\) of DMAA and \(\scriptstyle-\mathbf{N}-\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{2}-\) of NVCL respectively, which also confirmed that NVCL and DMAA had been successfully introduced into hydrogels. Furthermore, the FTIR spectra of the hydrogels with different MOF MIL-100(Fe) contents were shown in Fig. 3b. As can be seen, upon increasing the MOF MIL-100(Fe) content, the peak intensities at 760 and \(712\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) arising from the \(\mathrm{C{-}H}\) bending vibrations of benzene also increased. Besides, the appearance of a Fe 2p peak in the \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}10\%\) sample also confirmed the successful introduction of MOF MIL-100(Fe).
The thermal stabilities of the CNFs, SA, MIL-100 (Fe), NVCL, and the \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}0\%\) and \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}10\%\) hydrogels were then investigated by TGA (Fig. S3). As observed in the figure, NVCL had a low thermal stability with decomposition occurring between 110 and \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). While for the \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}0\%\) and \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}10\%\) hydrogels, decomposition peaks were observed between 375 and \(430\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) respectively. It can be interpreted as that the physical and chemical crosslinking between P(NVCL-DMAA) chains and cellulose limits the decomposition of cellulose and thereby improves the thermal stability of hydrogels. In addition, the \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}10\%\) hydrogel exhibited a higher thermal stability owing to its higher MOF MIL-100(Fe) content than \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}0\%\) hydrogel.
The morphologies of MOF MIL-100(Fe) and the \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}0\%\) and \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}10\%\) hydrogels were then investigated using SEM. Fig. 4a showed the macro physical images of the two hydrogels. The SEM image of MIL-100 (Fe) exhibited a large number of uniform and dense micropores (Fig. 4b), while the hydrogel without MOF MIL-100(Fe) possessed a smooth surface (Fig. 4c). Besides, the surface of the \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}10\%\) hydrogel (i.e., containing MIL-100(Fe)) showed an irregular micropore structure (Fig. 4d).
Moreover, the results of EDAX analysis (Fig. S4) indicated that MIL\(100(\mathrm{Fe})\) distributed homogeneously in the hydrogels. Thus, to verify whether MOF MIL-100(Fe) can function properly in hydrogels, the BET adsorption-desorption isotherms were obtained (Fig. 4f and Table S3). As indicated, the \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}10\%\) hydrogel exhibited a type II adsorption isotherm, as did MOF MIL-100(Fe) (Fig. S5). The \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}10\%\) hydrogel also possessed a higher specific surface area, thereby suggesting that the combination of MOF MIL-100(Fe) with hydrogels was beneficial to enhance the adsorption of fertilizers and water.
## 3.2. Swelling behavior and the swelling dynamic mechanism at room temperature
As a controlled release carrier for fertilizers and water, the swelling behavior and dynamic swelling mechanism should be explored in a
hydrogel. During the swelling process, the free adsorption of water occurred initially [43], as indicated in Fig. 5, where the swelling behaviors of the different hydrogels at room temperature were shown. The swelling rates of all MC hydrogels increased rapidly during the initial 6 h, and then increased slowly until equilibrium was reached. This was attributed to the expansion of the 3D network structure due to repulsion between the carboxylate groups in the hydrogels, which promoted the penetration of water into the 3D structure, and improved the swelling ratio. Compared with the \(\mathbf{MC-0\%}\) hydrogel, the \(\mathbf{MC-10\%}\) hydrogel showed the maximum increase in swelling capacity, i.e., from 27 to 37 \(\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{g}\) (Fig. 5b). This was due to the inhibiting of the physical entanglement of the CNFs and the SA chains by MOF MIL-100(Fe), which led to a reduction in the hydrogel cross-linking density and an increased swelling ratio. However, as the content of MOF MIL-100(Fe) was increased further (i.e., in the case of the \(\mathbf{MC-20\%}\) hydrogel), the swelling rate decreased. It was likely due to increased cross-linking, which limited expansion of the network structure and hindered the infiltration of water molecules. Fig. 5f showed the results of the hydrogel reuse experiment, from which their good ability to absorb and release water repeatedly can be clearly observed.
To explore the dynamic swelling mechanism of MC hydrogels in the initial stage, the Korsmeyer-Peppas model (Eq. (13)) and Schott's second-order swelling kinetics model (Eq. (14)) were introduced [41]. For Korsmeyer-Peppas model, \(w_{t}\) and \(w_{e}\) denoted the weight of water absorbed at any time t and at theoretical equilibrium respectively; \(K\) was a constant that describes the characteristic of hydrogels and n was the exponent that described the type of diffusion. Both \(K\) and \(n\) can be calculated from the plots of \(\log(\mathrm{f})\) versus \(\log(\mathrm{t})\) . For Schott's second-order swelling kinetics model, \(SR\) and \(ESR\) denoted the swelling ratios at any time \(t\) and at equilibrium respectively, and \(k\) represented the swelling rate constant.
\[
F=\frac{w_t}{w_e}=Kt^n
\]
\[
\frac{t}{SR}=\frac{1}{kESR^2}+\frac{1}{ESR}t
\]
As shown in Fig. 5d, good linear correlation coefficients were obtained \((\mathbf{R}^{2}>0.95)\) for the Korsmeyer-Peppas model, and the values of \(n\), \(K\), and \(\mathbf{R}^{2}\) were given in Table S4. According to the obtained values of \(n\) \((0.43<n<0.85)\), it can be concluded that the transport mechanism was non-Fickian diffusion. In addition, for Schott's second-order swelling kinetics model, the results presented in Fig. 5e confirmed the linear correlation coefficients \({>}0.99\), thereby indicating that this model was suitable for describing the MC hydrogel swelling behaviors. Moreover, the values of \(\mathrm{ESR}_{\mathrm{cal}}\) matched well with the experimental values \(\mathrm{(ESR_{exp})}\) (Table S4).
## 3.3. Temperature- and pH-responsive behaviors
It is well known that the conformations of the thermosensitive monomer NVCL chains in hydrogels change when the temperature is lower or higher than the low critical solution temperature (LCST), and this effect leads to the volume-phase transition of hydrogels [44]. More specifically, when the temperature is above the LCST, the hydrogels shrink rapidly. Thus, the prepared MC hydrogels were swollen in deionized water at various temperatures, and their equilibrium swelling ratios were calculated. As shown in Fig. 6a, the equilibrium swelling ratios of the MC hydrogels decreased as the temperature was increased, indicating that these MC hydrogels exhibited a high temperature response performance. In addition, the hydrogels were found to absorb water easily at temperatures below the LCST \((25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). It can therefore be interpreted that the PNVCL chains showed great hydrophilicity at temperatures below the LCST, but collapsed into a hydrophobic structure above the LCST [45] (Fig. S6a).
In the context of actual application, it is essential for hydrogels to achieve repeatable shrinkage and swelling. Therefore, the cycling properties of the different MC hydrogels in terms of their swelling and shrinking behaviors were investigated at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (below the LCST) and at \(55\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (above the LCST). As shown in Fig. 6b, all of the examined MC hydrogels exhibited good repeatable shrinkage and swelling behaviors, and showed a slightly consecutive reduction over time.
The pH-responsive behaviors of the MC hydrogels were subsequently evaluated by investigating the swelling ratios at various pH values. As shown in Fig. 6c, the swelling ratios of all hydrogels increased with the pH increasing from 3 to 11. From this result, it can be interpreted as that the –COOH groups dissociated into \(-\mathrm{COO}^{-}\)groups under alkaline conditions, thereby leading to an increase in the hydrophilicity and the electrostatic repulsion of the structure [46], and resulting in the observed improved water absorption ability (Fig. S6b). As shown in Fig. 6d, the examined MC hydrogels also exhibited good repeatable shrinkage and swelling behaviors at \(\mathrm{pH}=3\) and \(\mathrm{pH}=11\), presenting pleasuring pH-responsiveness.
Fig. 7a showed the water-retention capabilities of the different MC hydrogels in distilled water at \(55\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (above the LCST). As shown, the
introduction of MOF MIL-100(Fe) increased the water-retention of the hydrogels from \(60.28\%\) \((\mathrm{MC}{-}0\%)\) to \(77.53\%\) \((\mathrm{MC}{-}10\%)\). Moreover, the maximum water-holding capacity of \(\mathrm{MC}{-}10\%\) was found to be higher than that of \(\mathrm{MC}{-}0\%\) in a soil environment (Fig. S7). Besides, the sample mixed with \(\mathrm{MC}{-}10\%\) \(_{(1.0\mathrm{~g~})}\) presented the greatest water-retention capacity of the various samples examined (1.6, 7b), which still remained \(22.78\%\) in soil on day 30. For all the other samples, the water-retention reached to zero on day 30 or earlier. These observations suggested that the application of \(\mathrm{MC}{-}10\%\) hydrogels in agricultural production was a good choice to provide a suitable growth environment for crops.
## 3.4. Urea release behaviors of the MC hydrogels
To evaluate the application potentials of MC hydrogels in the field of slow-release fertilizers, the urea release behaviors of our prepared hydrogels were studies at different pH values and temperatures. Thus, Fig. S8 showed the FTIR spectra of MC hydrogels loaded with urea, and Fig. 8 showed the effects of pH and temperature on the cumulative release of urea. Overall, the obtained results illustrated a general trend wherein urea was released rapidly in the initial \(3\,\mathrm{h}\), followed by the subsequent slower release due to the fact that the urea loaded on the hydrogel surface was more easily released than that inside the hydrogel. Due to the pH responsiveness, the hydrogels exhibited lower fertilizer release rates in alkaline environments (Fig. 7a and b), so as to ensure a better fertilizer utilization efficiency in the environment (pH is around 7.5) where crops are suitable for growth. Similarly, a higher temperature (around \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) is beneficial to crops growth. Because of the temperature responsiveness, the hydrogels exhibited higher fertilizer release rates at the higher temperature (Fig. 8c and d), which also ensured sufficient nutrients for crops to sustain their growth. Overall, the \(\mathrm{MC}{-}10\%\) hydrogels presented the greatest slow-release performance under a range of conditions, and this was attributed to the fact that the \(\mathrm{MC}{-}10\%\) hydrogels possessed an abundant microporous structure and a tortuous diffusion path provided by MOF MIL-100(Fe) in the network, which inhibited the diffusion of urea, and improved its slow-release. To examine the release of urea in soil samples (Fig. S9), the cumulative release rates of \(\mathrm{MC}{-}0\%\) and \(\mathrm{MC}{-}10\%\) were determined, i.e., 49.74 and \(40.84\%\) on day 30, respectively, which indicated that \(\mathrm{MC}{-}10\%\) exhibited a better sustained release performance. Moreover, the measured nitrogen contents in the soil samples also confirmed the better slow-release capacities of the \(\mathrm{MC}{-}10\%\) hydrogels (Fig. S10).
The data displayed in Table 1 revealed the mechanisms involved in
the above release behaviors. According to the linear correlation coefficient \((\mathbf{R}^{2})\), the Higuchi model showed a good fit to the release data. In addition, based on the Korsmeyer-Peppas model and the calculated \(n\) values, as described above, the release mechanisms of the MC-0% and MC-10% hydrogels were determined to be non-Fickian and skeleton dissolution, respectively.
## 3.5. Practical applications
To explore the effect of the MC-10% hydrogel on the seed germination rates and nitrogen nutrition statuses of plants, wheat was used as a model crop to study the growth status under different fertilization methods. Thus, Fig. 9a showed the growth of wheat treated with different fertilization methods, from which it can be seen that the samples treated with the MC-10% and MC-0% hydrogels were significantly healthier than those treated with either no urea or free urea. In particular, the sample treated with the MC-10% hydrogel was more luxuriant than that treated with the MC-0% hydrogel. These results confirmed that the MC-10% hydrogel exhibited pleasuring water and fertilizer retention properties, and that its 3D structure and porous MIL100(Fe) component were beneficial to the growth of crops. Finally, we calculated the release percent of urea and its exhaustion of MC-10% hydrogels by measuring the remaining N content. During the whole process of wheat growth, the overall release percent of urea and its amount of exhaustion was 80%.
As previously reported, the amide structure, which is the main chemical structure of nitrogen in plants, can be divided into amide I and amide II groups [47]. The spectral region of amide II is in the range of 1500–1600 cm\(^{-1}\), and the signals in this region correspond to the C—N stretching vibrations and the N—H bending vibrations. The spectral region of amide I is 1600–1700 cm\(^{-1}\), and the signals in this region correspond to the C=O stretching vibration of the peptide bond. Thus, the nitrogen content of wheat can be assessed by examination of the
absorption bands belonging to amide I and amide II. Relative study has reported that the intensity ratio of amide II to amide I can well reflect the total N of the leaves [48]. Photocoastic spectroscopy was then used to investigate the absorption intensities of these signals in the crop leaves, and their corresponding ratios were calculated (Fig. 9b–e). As shown, the amide II/amide I ratio of the sample treated with the \(\mathbf{MC-10\%}\) hydrogel (0.72) was clearly the highest among the various samples, which was consistent with the wheat growth results discussed above.
The germination rate, tiller number, photosynthetic rate, and chlorophyll content of wheat were also tested for each sample, as outlined in Table S5. Compared with other groups, all values for the above properties were higher for the wheat treated with the \(\mathbf{MC-10\%}\) hydrogel, again indicating that the \(\mathbf{MC-10\%}\) hydrogel was beneficial in terms of continuously providing fertilizer and water to seeds, as well as promoting the growth of crops. In addition, the comparison of various parameters between this study and the relevant literature studies has been supplied as Table 2. Although the swelling capacity in this work was inferior, the water-retention and fertilizer slow-release performance of this study both performed well, especially the slow-release of fertilizer, which was also the reason for the use of MOFs. The fertilizers slow-release performance in soil of this study \((40.84\%)\) was better than that reported in other literature. Besides, the water-retention in soil of this study was also a level in the forefront \((22.78\%)\).
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we developed a kind of hydrogel to enhance the utilization rate of fertilizers and water by integrating MOF MIL-100(Fe) and cellulose-based hydrogels with temperature- and pH-responsiveness. Owing to the microporous structure of MOF MIL-100(Fe), the prepared \(\mathbf{MC-10\%}\) hydrogels exhibited higher water-retention and better urea slow-release performance. The water-retention increased from \(60.28\%\) \((\mathbf{MC-0\%})\) to \(77.53\%\) \((\mathbf{MC-10\%})\) in deionized water after \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) , and the cumulative release rates of \(\mathbf{MC-10\%}\) hydrogels reached to \(40.84\%\) in soil on day 30. Also, NVCL and CNFs provided temperatureand pH-sensitivities for regulating urea release. For the wheat sample treated with the \(\mathbf{MC-10\%}\) hydrogel, germination rate, tiller number, photosynthetic rate, and chlorophyll content were significantly increased.
